  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-47066" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Organization for Migration (IOM)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening sustainable solutions data and evidence through human security monitoring in the Sahel</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To strengthen the UN system&#x2019;s ability to support prevention, and improve Human Security, IOM proposes to capitalize on its existing human mobility tracking data and analyses products by adding a Human Security Module reflecting the perception of Human Security and current and future drivers of mobility to identify pockets of instability and fragility and monitoring of dynamics and drivers of vulnerability and mobility in the Sahel. The Human Security component would be collected through direct individual surveys - filling a gap in the granularity of the insights it can provide - and would be deployed and administered in partnership with a phone poll company - an on-demand insights platform built to accommodate the data capture requirements and standards of the international development community - to thousands of individuals across Sahelian countries allowing for fast data capturing, processing and analysis. The project will allow for two rounds of data collection on individuals&#x2019; perceptions of Human Security and potential drivers of mobility in the Sahel, which will, each, generate a distinct report. A positive or negative perception of Human Security directly impacts people&apos;s decision-making on their mobility (either chosen or forced). The Human Security Module would include similar questions already collected by the SMI through KIs around economic, political, food, health, environmental, community and personal security, shedding light on what variables have the most significant impacts the perceptions of human security and mobility/migration decisions. Distinct from the SMI, this component would feed into the SMI work and subsequent analysis, allowing for a more detailed horizon scanning of migration drivers and vulnerability hotspots.</narrative>
  </description>
